<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tulsidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1532–<a href="/years/1623">1623</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tulsi Das, Tulasidasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tulsidas was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> poet-saint of 16th-century northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, devoted to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, who spent most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> schooled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, he is best known for the Ramcharitmanas, a retelling of the Sanskrit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in the vernacular Awadhi, which he began at Ayodhya in 1574. Popular belief also makes him the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a>, and he is credited with founding the Sankat Mochan Hanuman Temple in Varanasi and with starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> plays, a folk-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> version of the Ramayana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tulsidas.jpg" alt="Tulsidas" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nawal Kishore Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustration_of_Tuls%C4%ABd%C4%81s_holding_a_M%C4%81nas_manuscript_in_front_of_an_audience,_Naval_Kishore_edition,_1882.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tulsidas was born Rambola Dubey, on the seventh day of the bright half of the month of Shraavana. The exact date and year of his birth are disputed among scholars and biographers. Tulsidas himself gives only a few facts about his life, and the early sources are the Bhaktamal of Nabhadas, a contemporary who calls him an incarnation of Valmiki.</p><p>Many believe he was a reincarnation of Valmiki. According to the Tulsi Prakash and some other works he married Ratnavali, and after she rebuked him for his attachment to her he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayag</a> and became a sadhu. Most stories about him are apocryphal and have been carried forward by word of mouth, since none was related by Tulsidas himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After renunciation he spent most of his time at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayag</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> and Chitrakuta, and travelled to many other places. Tradition holds that he began composing poetry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in Varanasi, but that the verses he wrote by day were lost each night.</p><p>On the eighth night <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> is believed to have ordered him in a dream to compose in the vernacular and to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> to write in Awadhi. He is generally believed to have died in 1623 at Assi Ghat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, though the exact day and month are disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Twelve works are widely considered to be his, six major and six minor, in Awadhi and Braj. The Ramcharitmanas, composed between 1574 and 1576, is his longest and earliest work, of around 12,800 lines in seven books, about one third the size of Valmiki's Ramayana. His other works include the Dohavali, the Vinaya Patrika, the Gitavali, and the Parvati Mangal and Janaki Mangal, on the marriages of Parvati and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a> is popularly attributed to him and bears his signature, though some authors doubt he wrote it. In his teaching Tulsidas holds that the Nirguna Brahman, without qualities, and the Saguna Brahman, with qualities, are one and the same, but he favours the Saguna aspect. Repeating the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> is, in his view, the only means to reach God in the Kali age.</p><p>He sees no incompatibility between devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and attachment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and his writings aim to teach bhakti to Rama as the means of salvation, free and open to men of the lowest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>. The Tulsi Ghat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> is named after him, and the Sankat Mochan Hanuman Temple there is believed to stand where he saw Hanuman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tulsidas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…ascribed to the poet Valmiki, tells of the exile of Prince Rama, the abduction of his wife Sita by the demon king Ravana and her rescue; retold in countless versions, among them the Ramcharitmanas of <b>Tulsidas</b> in the 16th century, it is performed across South and Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tulsidas</b> recorded in the Ramcharitmanas itself that he began the poem at Ayodhya in 1574, on the festival of Rama’s birth; the Ramlila plays still staged across northern India each autumn are based on its verses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Brahmin priests and scholars performed the rites of temple and household and kept Sanskrit learning alive; the Brahmin poet <b>Tulsidas</b> retold the Ramayana in Awadhi.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hanuman Chalisa, &quot;forty chaupais on Hanuman&quot;, is a Hindu devotional hymn (stotra) in praise of Hanuman, written in Awadhi by <b>Tulsidas</b>, a poet-saint of the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a></li><li><blockquote>It is believed to have been written by the poet <b>Tulsidas</b> or drawn from his Ramcharitmanas, or to come from a 17th-century sung prayer by the Marathi saint-poet Ramdas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/raghupati-raghava-raja-ram">Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rajapur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Assi Ghat, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.38,81.15&amp;year=1591" aria-label="Rajapur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6699 1230 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tulsidas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7149 1500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7149 1500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.38,81.15&amp;year=1591">What was here in 1591? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
